INT. BOXING GYM, BASEMENT OF A BAKERY - NIGHT

Low ceiling. Flour dust drifts through the light of a single bulb. The ring is eight feet square, ropes tied with bakery twine. Heat from the ovens above rattles the pipes.

YELENA, twenty-six, a welterweight with a taped nose, sits on her corner stool, chest heaving. COACH THANDIWE, fifty, built like a door, kneels in front of her with a water bottle. Across the ring, KIONA, twenty-four, the challenger, shakes out her gloves. SVEN, sixty, the referee, in a flour-white shirt, checks his watch.

THANDIWE
Look at me. She's dropping her right hand.

YELENA
I see it.

THANDIWE
Do you? She's tired and she thinks you are too.

YELENA
She's right.

THANDIWE
She's wrong. Breathe.

Yelena breathes. Thandiwe squeezes water onto her mouthpiece.

SVEN
Ladies. Time.

Thandiwe stands. Yelena rises. Across the ring, Kiona waits, bouncing on her toes.

THANDIWE
Last round. She comes in, you cut left. She backs up, you let her.

The bell, a dented cake pan struck with a spoon, rings.
